-- CONTROL for #1177.  Byte-identical to main.mdk except that the `=>` context reads
-- `(Ix a Char, Dbg a)` instead of `(Dbg a, Ix a Char)` -- the two predicates SWAPPED,
-- a reordering that changes no program meaning.  That single change flips both engines
-- to the correct 227, which is the defect stated as an experiment.
--
-- Per #1144 this harness grades a control on its EXIT CODE ONLY, and a
-- wrong-value-at-exit-0 bug cannot be discriminated by an exit code unless the program
-- puts the value INTO the exit code.  So `main` ASSERTS 227 and panics otherwise,
-- rather than printing it -- a control that merely printed would exit 0 either way and
-- could never fail.
--
-- ⚠️ THE CONTROL IS ALSO A CLAIM ABOUT WHICH DIMENSION MATTERS.  It is NOT enough that
-- 227 appears somewhere: what it isolates is that the ONLY thing changed is the
-- position of `Ix a Char` in the context.  The impls, their order, the bodies and the
-- call are all identical to main.mdk.  A second, independent isolation is recorded in
-- the issue: giving the two predicates DISTINCT type variables also yields 227,
-- because the collapse is keyed on the shared tyvar id.
interface Ix a b where
  ix : a -> b -> Int

impl Ix Int Bool where
  ix _ _ = 111

impl Ix Int Char where
  ix _ _ = 222

interface Dbg a where
  dbg : a -> Int

impl Dbg Int where
  dbg n = n

useIx : (Ix a Char, Dbg a) => a -> Int
useIx x = ix x 'z' + dbg x

main =
  let r = useIx 5
  if r == 227 then
    println "control-ok:227"
  else
    panic "control regressed: moving `Ix a Char` to the front of the context no longer yields the correct 227"
